    "problem_statement": "Path.load() returns None instead of self\n\n#### Description\n\nWhen calling `Path.load()`, the method returns `None` instead of returning `self` for method chaining. This breaks the fluent interface pattern used throughout the library.\n\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\n\n```python\nfrom furl import furl\n\n# This should work but returns None\npath = furl.Path()\nresult = path.load('/some/path')\nprint(result)  # Prints: None (should print the Path object)\n\n# Method chaining is broken\nf = furl.furl()\n# This will fail because load() returns None\nf.path.load('/test').add('more')\n```\n\n#### Expected Behavior\n\n`Path.load()` should return `self` to allow method chaining, similar to other methods in the library like `add()`, `set()`, etc.\n\n#### Actual Behavior\n\n`Path.load()` returns `None`, breaking method chaining and the fluent interface pattern."
